WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.220
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.580 --> 00:00:05.220
In our previous class, a complete current loop in a uniform magnetic field had

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:06.100
zero net force.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.460
Today, that same loop can turn.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.680
How can both statements be true?

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.620
Imagine opening a jar lid with two fingers pushing in opposite directions at

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:16.280
different points.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.520
The pushes can cancel as forces while adding as a turning effect.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:21.940
That is a couple.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:26.860
Take a rectangular loop of sides a and b carrying steady current in a uniform

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:27.580
external field.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.996
At the orientation where the field lies in the loop's plane, two opposite

00:00:31.996 --> 00:00:32.260
sides

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:35.600
of length b each feel force I b B.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.120
Their forces are opposite, but their lines of action are separated by a.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:45.540
The torque is force times that separation, giving I a b B, or I A B.

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.160
The other two sides are parallel to the field in this orientation, so their

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.320
magnetic forces vanish.

00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:51.860
Tilt the loop.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.320
If theta is the angle between its normal and B, the couple's effective arm

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:57.460
becomes a sine theta.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.840
The torque magnitude becomes I A B sine theta.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.840
At ninety degrees between normal and field, torque is largest.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.640
At zero degrees it vanishes.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.640
Be careful: a normal is a line perpendicular to the loop's plane.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.020
Maximum torque occurs when the plane is parallel to B, not when the plane is

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:15.980
perpendicular to B.

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:20.240
Zero net force means no translational acceleration from these magnetic forces;

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:20.712
it does not mean no rotational acceleration.

00:01:20.712 --> 00:01:25.152
We can package a loop's area, current and orientation into one useful vector:

00:01:25.772 --> 00:01:27.112
its magnetic dipole moment.

00:01:27.792 --> 00:01:32.012
For a single planar loop, its magnitude is I times enclosed area A.

00:01:32.672 --> 00:01:34.512
Its direction is the right hand normal.

00:01:35.472 --> 00:01:38.952
Curl your right fingers along conventional current around the loop; your thumb

00:01:38.952 --> 00:01:40.472
points along the dipole moment.

00:01:41.492 --> 00:01:45.252
Viewed from your side of the page, counterclockwise current gives a moment out

00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:45.852
of the page.

00:01:46.732 --> 00:01:48.952
Clockwise current gives a moment into the page.

00:01:49.752 --> 00:01:52.852
The moment points perpendicular to the plane, not along the wire.

00:01:53.872 --> 00:01:58.042
For N identical turns with their normals aligned, add the moments to get N I

00:01:58.042 --> 00:01:58.152
A.

00:01:59.072 --> 00:02:03.252
Area means the area of one turn; multiplying an already summed area by N again

00:02:03.252 --> 00:02:04.452
would count the turns twice.

00:02:04.932 --> 00:02:06.712
The unit is ampere meter squared.

00:02:07.552 --> 00:02:11.692
Suppose a coil has one hundred turns, current zero point two amperes, and area

00:02:11.692 --> 00:02:13.372
forty square centimeters per turn.

00:02:14.252 --> 00:02:17.192
Convert the area to zero point zero zero four square meters.

00:02:17.932 --> 00:02:22.218
Its moment is one hundred times zero point two times zero point zero zero

00:02:22.218 --> 00:02:22.512
four,

00:02:22.512 --> 00:02:25.112
or zero point zero eight ampere meter squared.

00:02:25.752 --> 00:02:27.492
The torque vector is moment cross B.

00:02:28.132 --> 00:02:31.432
This gives both the sine angle magnitude and the turning axis direction.

00:02:32.112 --> 00:02:36.012
If the moment is out of the page and B points right, the torque vector points

00:02:36.012 --> 00:02:36.932
upward in the page.

00:02:37.712 --> 00:02:41.232
Reversing the current reverses the moment and torque for the same field and

00:02:41.232 --> 00:02:41.912
orientation.

00:02:42.732 --> 00:02:45.952
Keep the right hand grip rule for the moment separate from the cross product

00:02:45.952 --> 00:02:46.672
rule for torque.

00:02:46.872 --> 00:02:50.832
Let's use that coil in a field of zero point five zero tesla.

00:02:51.692 --> 00:02:56.559
Its moment is zero point zero eight ampere meter squared, so moment times

00:02:56.559 --> 00:02:56.892
field

00:02:56.892 --> 00:02:59.552
is zero point zero four zero newton meters.

00:02:59.972 --> 00:03:01.512
That is the maximum torque.

00:03:01.832 --> 00:03:06.652
If the moment makes thirty degrees with B, multiply by sine thirty, which is

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:07.092
one half.

00:03:07.592 --> 00:03:10.432
The torque is zero point zero two zero newton meters.

00:03:10.852 --> 00:03:12.532
Now change only the wording.

00:03:12.632 --> 00:03:16.692
The question says the plane of the coil makes sixty degrees with the field.

00:03:17.392 --> 00:03:17.952
What do we do?

00:03:18.872 --> 00:03:20.592
Pause and draw a short normal to the plane.

00:03:21.292 --> 00:03:25.692
For this acute plane angle, the normal makes thirty degrees with B, so the

00:03:25.692 --> 00:03:28.612
answer is still zero point zero two zero newton meters.

00:03:29.332 --> 00:03:33.612
If the plane instead makes thirty degrees with B, the normal angle is sixty

00:03:33.612 --> 00:03:34.012
degrees.

00:03:34.832 --> 00:03:37.852
Torque is about zero point zero three four six newton meters.

00:03:38.112 --> 00:03:42.452
You may write the magnitude using cosine of the acute plane angle, but do not

00:03:42.452 --> 00:03:45.512
mix that cosine with an angle labeled between the normal and B.

00:03:46.052 --> 00:03:50.332
And remember that torque is measured in newton meters, while dipole moment is

00:03:50.332 --> 00:03:51.752
measured in ampere meter squared.

00:03:52.472 --> 00:03:57.372
Another common slip is area conversion: forty square centimeters is not zero

00:03:57.372 --> 00:03:58.412
point four square meters.

00:03:59.172 --> 00:04:03.012
Each centimeter contributes a factor of one hundredth, so a square centimeter

00:04:03.012 --> 00:04:05.272
is ten to the minus four square meters.

00:04:06.232 --> 00:04:10.892
Identify the angle, convert the area, find the moment, and only then calculate

00:04:10.892 --> 00:04:11.252
torque.

00:04:11.424 --> 00:04:14.044
Torque tells us how the coil starts to turn.

00:04:14.984 --> 00:04:17.504
Potential energy tells us which orientations are favored.

00:04:18.284 --> 00:04:22.134
For a fixed magnetic dipole moment in a prescribed uniform field,

00:04:22.134 --> 00:04:22.904
orientational

00:04:22.904 --> 00:04:26.544
energy is minus moment times field times cosine theta.

00:04:27.224 --> 00:04:29.544
We choose zero energy at ninety degrees.

00:04:30.124 --> 00:04:34.384
With our moment times field equal to zero point zero four joules, parallel

00:04:34.384 --> 00:04:37.264
alignment has energy minus zero point zero four joules.

00:04:37.984 --> 00:04:40.864
Antiparallel alignment has plus zero point zero four joules.

00:04:41.724 --> 00:04:45.547
Both positions have zero torque exactly, because sine zero and sine one

00:04:45.547 --> 00:04:45.924
hundred

00:04:45.924 --> 00:04:46.844
eighty are zero.

00:04:47.644 --> 00:04:48.884
But are they equally stable?

00:04:49.704 --> 00:04:54.264
A small tilt away from parallel alignment produces a restoring torque; it is

00:04:54.264 --> 00:04:54.524
stable.

00:04:55.244 --> 00:04:59.004
A small tilt away from antiparallel alignment pushes the dipole farther from

00:04:59.004 --> 00:05:00.804
that position; it is unstable.

00:05:01.644 --> 00:05:04.884
Without damping, a displaced dipole need not settle quietly.

00:05:05.304 --> 00:05:07.544
It can oscillate around the stable direction.

00:05:08.484 --> 00:05:12.371
Now slowly rotate the fixed moment dipole from sixty degrees to one hundred

00:05:12.371 --> 00:05:14.524
twenty degrees, keeping B unchanged.

00:05:15.464 --> 00:05:20.044
Initial energy is minus zero point zero two joules; final energy is plus zero

00:05:20.044 --> 00:05:20.984
point zero two joules.

00:05:21.724 --> 00:05:25.504
The external agent supplies zero point zero four joules of quasistatic work.

00:05:26.104 --> 00:05:29.644
The magnetic torque does the opposite amount of mechanical work in this ideal

00:05:29.644 --> 00:05:30.524
orientation model.

00:05:31.184 --> 00:05:35.164
For a maintained current coil, an electrical source participates in the energy

00:05:35.164 --> 00:05:35.544
balance.

00:05:36.224 --> 00:05:40.464
This is not a claim that magnetic force alone increases an isolated charge's

00:05:40.464 --> 00:05:40.824
kinetic energy.

00:05:41.624 --> 00:05:44.824
Keep the model's energy accounting distinct from the single particle rule we

00:05:44.824 --> 00:05:45.384
learned earlier.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.400
Finish with a design style JEE comparison.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:54.240
The same forty centimeter wire can form either one square turn or one circular

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:56.340
turn, with negligible lead length.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.060
Current is two amperes in each case.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:04.267
For the square, each side is ten centimeters, so enclosed area is zero point

00:06:04.267 --> 00:06:05.400
zero one square meters.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.200
The moment is zero point zero two ampere meter squared.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.560
For the circle, circumference is the wire length.

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:17.440
Radius is the length divided by two pi, and area is length squared divided by

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:17.900
four pi.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:22.080
Its moment is about zero point zero two five five ampere meter squared.

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:26.900
Dividing the circle result by the square result gives four over pi, about one

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:28.360
point two seven.

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:31.900
The circle has the larger moment and larger maximum torque in the same field.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:35.800
Notice what stayed fixed: current, wire length and one turn.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:40.151
Saying more turns always means a bigger moment can be misleading when each

00:06:40.151 --> 00:06:40.340
turn

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:40.660
shrinks.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:45.720
If the same wire forms N equal circular turns with negligible leads, each

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:48.000
circumference is the total length divided by N.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:52.640
Each turn's area falls as one over N squared, so the total moment falls as one

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:53.860
over N at fixed current.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.720
Two such turns have half the single turn moment.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.760
That does not contradict moment equal to N I A; it changes A while changing N.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:05.320
Before using a proportionality, write what is held constant.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:09.440
We now have the foundation for the moving coil galvanometer, which will be our

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:09.980
next class.
